theory.intro:
One **radian** is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc equal in length to the radius. Because a full turn has circumference $C = 2\pi r$, a complete revolution is $2\pi\text{ radians}$ and $180^{\circ} = \pi\text{ radians}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (3,0);
\coordinate (B) at (1.62,2.52);
\draw[thick] (O) -- (A) node[midway, below] {$r$};
\draw[thick] (O) -- (B) node[midway, above left] {$r$};
\draw[thick] (A) arc (0:57.3:3) node[midway, right] {$l = r$};
\pic[draw, "$1\text{ rad}$", angle radius=1.0cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle=A--O--B};
\node[left] at (O) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

